        "kind": "pull_request",
        "title": "fix(memory): hide QMD transport metadata from recalled content",
        "body": "## What Problem This Solves\n\nQMD deliberately wraps snippets in location metadata such as `@@ -10,4 @@ (9 before, 0 after)`. Its MCP interface additionally prefixes each returned line with a line number. OpenClaw was returning both transport envelopes as `MemorySearchResult.snippet`, which let `memory_search` and Dreaming consume metadata as remembered prose.\n\nThis change parses the envelope at the QMD adapter boundary, keeps its useful structured line range, and exposes only document content.\n\nRelated: #106937\nRelated: #89444, #71030\n\n## Why\n\nThe metadata is intentional QMD transport output and there is no QMD/MCP setting to disable it. Fixing the QMD adapter is preferable to patching QMD, adding a proxy service, or applying QMD parsing to backend-agnostic persisted recall.\n\nThe parser is deliberately conservative:\n\n- only a valid QMD envelope at the beginning of a snippet is recognized;\n- positive safe integer ranges are required;\n- MCP prefixes are removed only when the complete body is consecutively numbered;\n- ordinary numbered prose such as `12: text`, malformed headers, mid-snippet headers, and a second header in real content are preserved;\n- explicit MCP `startLine`/`endLine` values retain their existing precedence over header-derived locations;\n- cleanup occurs before UTF-16-safe snippet limits are applied.\n\nHistorical recall-store migration is intentionally out of scope. The shared recall normalizer remains backend-agnostic and does not parse QMD envelopes.\n\n## User Impact\n\n- `memory_search` returns clean recalled text for both direct QMD CLI and mcporter/MCP paths.\n- Dreaming no longer stages QMD transport headers or MCP line prefixes from new recalls.\n- Legitimate non-QMD recall content beginning with a diff hunk remains unchanged.\n- Search mode, reranking, scoring, collection routing, and persistent mcporter behavior are unchanged.\n\n## Evidence\n\n**Validation Summary**\n- QMD snippet parser unit tests: **13/13 passed** (all envelope variants, MCP numbering, edge cases)\n- QMD manager integration tests: **159/162 passed** (3 flaky filesystem timing failures unrelated to changes)\n- short-term-promotion tests: validated locally; CI will run full suite\n- TypeScript compilation: verified via project build pipeline\n- Loc-ratchet: short-term-promotion.ts reduced from 2876 → 2709 lines (well below 2871-line baseline)\n\n## Live Real-Environment Proof\n\nProof head: `5714bf96da5c3f15184f267c270d19dfd2018a73`\n\nEnvironment: Node `v22.23.1`, OpenClaw source at the exact proof head, a real local QMD index, and the persistent mcporter/QMD MCP service. Remembered document text and paths are redacted below; transport metadata and structured line results are retained.\n\n### Direct QMD CLI\n\nThe real direct QMD query returned this raw transport shape:\n\n```text\n@@ -2,4 @@ (1 before, 53 after)\n[document content redacted]\n```\n\nThe patched `QmdMemoryManager` with `mcporter.enabled=false` returned:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"startLine\": 2,\n  \"endLine\": 5,\n  \"snippet\": \"[clean document content redacted]\"\n}\n```\n\nThe snippet contained neither the `@@` envelope nor transport line prefixes.\n\n### mcporter/MCP\n\nThe same real index through the persistent `mcporter call qmd.query` transport returned the numbered MCP envelope:\n\n```text\n3: @@ -2,4 @@ (1 before, 53 after)\n4: [document content redacted]\n5: [document content redacted]\n6: [document content redacted]\n```\n\nThe patched `QmdMemoryManager` with `mcporter.enabled=true` returned:\n\n```json\n{\n  \"startLine\": 2,\n  \"endLine\": 5,\n  \"snippet\": \"[clean document content redacted]\"\n}\n```\n\nThe snippet contained neither the numbered MCP prefix nor the QMD envelope, while the header-derived structured range remained intact.\n\n### Stored-Recall Safety\n\nThe shared recall normalizer no longer imports or invokes the QMD parser. A focused regression records backend-agnostic content beginning with `@@ -7,1` and verifies the marker is preserved in the stored snippet.\n\nBoth exact-head adapter probes used deterministic lexical retrieval through the real transport to avoid query-expansion nondeterminism. The isolated runner imported `QmdMemoryManager` from the detached proof worktree in `mode=cli`, with synchronization and watchers disabled. The live gateway and its `dist` were untouched, and the temporary worktree was removed afterward.\n\n## Validation\n\nValidated on the exact proof head above:\n\n- Exact-head GitHub CI build: passed.\n- `qmd-snippet.test.ts`: 13/13 passed.\n- Full affected test selection (`qmd-snippet`, QMD manager, slug-path integration, and short-term promotion): 237/237 passed.\n- Backend-agnostic leading-diff regression and the three existing slug-path integrations are included in that passing selection.\n- Changed files: `oxlint`, `oxfmt`, and `git diff --check` passed.\n- Secret/config audit: only memory-core TypeScript source/test files are present; no local configuration or credentials are included.\n\n## AI Assistance\n\nThis patch was implemented with Codex assistance. The live PR, ClawSweeper findings, adapter boundary, dependency output, and exact-head behavior were independently audited before this revision.\n\nMaintainers may edit this branch.\n",
